  "answer_text": "When we talk about \"clean gold\" or \"pure gold\" (typically 24-karat gold), it is represented as **99.9%** (or **99.99%** for high-grade investment bullion) rather than 100%. \n\nIn the physical world, **absolute 100% purity is scientifically, chemically, and practically impossible to achieve.** \n\nSeveral key barriers prevent gold from ever reaching 100% purity:\n\n### 1. Chemical and Refining Limitations\nGold is found in nature mixed with other elements (like silver, copper, iron, and platinum). To clean it, refiners use two main chemical processes:\n*   **The Miller Process:** Uses chlorine gas to filter out impurities, bringing gold to about **99.5%** purity.\n*   **The Wohlwill Process:** An electrochemical process that refines the gold further, achieving **99.99%** (known as \"four nines\" fine) or even **99.999%** purity.\n\nEven with these highly advanced processes, separating every single foreign atom from the gold is impossible. Some microscopic traces of other metals will always remain trapped within the gold\u2019s crystalline lattice.\n\n### 2. The Law of Diminishing Returns (Thermodynamics)\nAccording to the laws of thermodynamics, as a substance gets closer to absolute purity, the energy and effort required to remove the remaining impurities increase **exponentially**. \nRemoving the last 0.1% or 0.01% of impurities is extremely difficult, and attempting to remove the final few foreign atoms would require highly specialized laboratory conditions and a massive, economically unviable amount of energy. Because a 99.9% pure gold bar behaves physically and chemically exactly like a hypothetical 100% pure bar, there is no practical reason to spend millions of dollars trying to remove the last few trace atoms.\n\n### 3. Immediate Environmental Contamination\nEven if scientists managed to create a theoretically perfect \"100% pure\" gold sample in a vacuum, it would not stay that way for long. The moment the gold is poured into a mold, touched by tools, or exposed to the air, it instantly bonds with microscopic contaminants. Airborne dust, gases, and microscopic oils from human hands or storage containers immediately settle on the gold's surface, instantly dropping its purity below 100%.\n\n### 4. Limits of Scientific Measurement\nFrom a scientific standpoint, it is impossible to *prove* a substance is 100.00% pure. Modern testing equipment (like X-ray fluorescence or mass spectrometers) has limits of detection. They can only measure down to parts per million or parts per billion. Because scientists cannot test for \"zero\" impurities with absolute mathematical certainty, labeling gold as 100% pure would be scientifically dishonest. Labeling it 99.9% or 99.99% is the most accurate, verifiable representation of the metal. \n\n***\n\n**Fun Fact:** The closest humanity has ever come to absolute purity was in 1957, when the Perth Mint in Australia refined a plate of gold to **999.999 fineness** (99.9999% pure), often referred to as \"six nines\". Even this historic achievement was still 0.0001% away from being perfectly pure!",
